DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssttaacckk KPI allows querying of kernel stack trace information and the
     automated generation of kernel stack trace strings for the purposes of
     debugging and tracing.  To use the KPI, at least one of ooppttiioonnss DDDDBB and
     ooppttiioonnss SSTTAACCKK must be compiled into the kernel.

     Each stack trace is described by a _s_t_r_u_c_t _s_t_a_c_k.  It can be declared in
     the usual ways, including on the stack, and optionally initialized with
     ssttaacckk__zzeerroo(), though this is not necessary before saving a trace.  It can
     also be dynamically allocated with ssttaacckk__ccrreeaattee().  The _f_l_a_g_s argument is
     passed to malloc(9).  This dynamic allocation must be freed with
     ssttaacckk__ddeessttrrooyy().

     A trace of the current thread's kernel call stack may be captured using
     ssttaacckk__ssaavvee().  ssttaacckk__ssaavvee__ttdd() can be used to capture the kernel stack of
     a caller-specified thread.  Callers of ssttaacckk__ssaavvee__ttdd() must own the
     thread lock of the specified thread, and the thread's stack must not be
     swapped out.  ssttaacckk__ssaavvee__ttdd() can capture the kernel stack of a running
     thread, though note that this is not implemented on all platforms.  If
     the thread is running, the caller must also hold the process lock for the
     target thread.

     ssttaacckk__pprriinntt() and ssttaacckk__pprriinntt__sshhoorrtt() may be used to print a stack trace
     using the kernel printf(9), and may sleep as a result of acquiring sx(9)
     locks in the kernel linker while looking up symbol names.  In locking-
     sensitive environments, the unsynchronized ssttaacckk__pprriinntt__ddddbb() and
     ssttaacckk__pprriinntt__sshhoorrtt__ddddbb() variants may be invoked.  This function bypasses
     kernel linker locking, making it usable in ddb(4), but not in a live sys‐
     tem where linker data structures may change.

     ssttaacckk__ssbbuuff__pprriinntt() may be used to construct a human-readable string, in‐
     cluding conversion (where possible) from a simple kernel instruction
     pointer to a named symbol and offset.  The argument _s_b must be an ini‐
     tialized struct sbuf as described in sbuf(9).  This function may sleep if
     an auto-extending struct sbuf is used, or due to kernel linker locking.
     In locking-sensitive environments, such as ddb(4), the unsynchronized
     ssttaacckk__ssbbuuff__pprriinntt__ddddbb() variant may be invoked to avoid kernel linker
     locking; it should be used with a fixed-length sbuf.

     The utility functions ssttaacckk__zzeerroo, ssttaacckk__ccooppyy, and ssttaacckk__ppuutt may be used
     to manipulate stack data structures directly.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     ssttaacckk__ppuutt() returns 0 on success.  Otherwise the struct stack does not
     contain space to record additional frames, and a non-zero value is re‐
     turned.

     ssttaacckk__ssaavvee__ttdd() returns 0 when the stack capture was successful and a
     non-zero error number otherwise.  In particular, EBUSY is returned if the
     thread was running in user mode at the time that the capture was at‐
     tempted, and EOPNOTSUPP is returned if the operation is not implemented.

